Now discuss benefits of tail recursion in this context:

- Tail recursion allows the compiler to transform the recursive call into a loop (reuse the same stack frame). This avoids stack overflow for large n, because the recursion depth is limited by the tail recursion optimization. Without tail recursion, the naive recursive fib (fib(n) = fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)) would have exponential time and huge stack depth, causing stack overflow for even moderate n.

- Tail recursion ensures constant stack space (O(1)) regardless of recursion depth. So computing large n (e.g., n=100000) is feasible without stack overflow, though BigInt arithmetic may be slow for huge numbers.

- Performance: The tail-recursive version runs in O(n) time and O(1) space (excluding the size of the numbers themselves which grow). The iterative version would be similar; tail recursion is just a functional way to write an iterative algorithm. The compiler can optimize it to a jump, making it as efficient as a while loop. In Scala, the @tailrec annotation enforces that the function is indeed tail-recursive, catching mistakes at compile time.

- Contrast with naive recursion: O(2^n) time and O(n) stack space, impractical.

